WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
Weak authentication lets attackers take over accounts through reused passwords,

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.700
poor recovery flows, weak enrollment controls, or inconsistent enforcement.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.960
Payment administration and CDE-impacting access require special attention.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.620
MFA failures happen when multifactor authentication is missing, optional, bypassable,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.560
or undermined by weak recovery processes.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.900
Exceptions, remembered devices, and administrative resets should be tightly governed.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.080
Session management controls whether authenticated access remains trustworthy after login.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.900
Tokens should be protected, rotated when needed, invalidated on logout or major changes,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.800
and resistant to replay.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.340
Credential attacks such as credential stuffing, password spraying,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.880
and brute force attempts require monitoring, rate limits, lockout logic,

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.500
risk-based checks, and alerting.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.880
Use multifactor authentication where required and for high-risk access.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.860
Apply strong password and authenticator management practices.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.040
Protect sessions with secure token handling and reauthentication for sensitive actions.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:14.060
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req. 8.2 - Authentication controls; Req.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:21.080
8.3 - Multi-factor authentication; Req. 8.6 - Credential management.
